  <data key="d7">[{"pat_id": "US-4062376-A", "para_id": "para_34", "text": "with the service conduit 27 and the leakage conduit 26 to be installed connected to the ends of the flexible wedge 52 the two conduits are moved through the ground when the snake 51 is pulled with the ground between their proximate ends being radially compacted with the rate at which the conduits may be moved depending in part on the nature of the soil"}, {"pat_id": "US-4944872-A", "para_id": "para_172", "text": "also the conduit system serves as a transporting system for bringing the material to the processing facility instead of requiring separate transport"}, {"pat_id": "US-4969393-A", "para_id": "para_32", "text": "an advantage of the water softening unit 22 and of the monitoring means of fig"}, {"pat_id": "US-11535612-B2", "para_id": "para_134", "text": "it is therefore possible to provide a device equipped with a first turbine particularly suitable to be positioned in a ventilation conduit or at the end of this conduit opening into a kitchen and therefore having a relatively high flow rate and therefore an air speed and to provide a second turbine different from the first one particularly suitable for placement in a ventilation conduit opening into a bathroom and therefore having a relatively low air flow and speed"}, {"pat_id": "US-3895496-A", "para_id": "claim_1", "text": "a hand tool for the manual setting to grade of consecutive flexible conduits below the ground surface said tool comprising an elongate rigid member a series of clips depending at intervals from said elongate member each of said clips being of a highly flexible nature and in a normal unflexed state having a bite of slightly lesser dimension than a crossectional dimension of the conduit said clips having outwardly directed end segments contactable with the conduit during a closing movement between the tool and conduit an arcuate plate attached to one end of said elongate member for rested placement on the end of a previously laid conduit to align a clip supported conduit with a previously laid conduit a level located adjacent the other end of said rigid member for indicating to the worker the grade of the rigid member and hence the grade of the mounted flexible conduit an adjustable support structure attached to the other end of said rigid member to permit support of the conduit at a desired grade while the worker backfills the conduit receiving trench and said clips being disengageable from the conduit therein by the manual lifting of the elongate rigid member in the presence of backfill material bearing on the conduit"}, {"pat_id": "EP-2404151-B1", "para_id": "para_6", "text": "thus there is a need in this industry and others for reliable and cost effective systems and methods to predict and detect blockages and breakages in a sewer system other fluid delivery system or other gas delivery system such that preventative and emergency maintenance may be performed with an efficient deployment of resources and major damages and losses may be prevented.the document us 2003 033879 a1 describes monitoring a conduit by transmitting a signal in the conduit in a direction parallel to an intended fluid flow direction and by sensing a blockage in the associated conduit section based on a reflected signal measured with sensors disposed at the conduit"}, {"pat_id": "US-4650367-A", "para_id": "para_45", "text": "the soil load transferred to a rigid conduit may be several times that imposed on a flexible conduit with a compressibility equal to the adjacent soil"}, {"pat_id": "US-4650367-A", "para_id": "para_85", "text": "creep problems are avoided because plastics specifically pvc are known to be less susceptible to creep phenomena when loaded in compression"}, {"pat_id": "US-9488316-B2", "para_id": "para_55", "text": "because of the porous nature of porous conduit 130 and its selected intentionally manufactured even porosity the porous conduit 130 provides effectively hundreds thousands or millions of spaced locations e.g"}, {"pat_id": "US-4596491-A", "para_id": "para_45", "text": "the soil load transferred to a rigid conduit may be several times that imposed on a flexible conduit with a compressibility equal to the adjacent soil"}, {"pat_id": "US-4596491-A", "para_id": "para_85", "text": "creep problems are avoided because plastics specifically pvc are known to be less susceptible to creep phenomena when loaded in compression"}]</data>
