ture is detected the method includes marking the particular floor associated with the noticeable increase in the temperature on the property as the area of the property to be used to access the detectable underground cold water pipeline containing the leak for any future repair or replacement of the detectable underground cold water pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_42", "text": "the system can also be used to identify which pipe to replace based on a change in temperature of a pipe entering the ground point a from a specific first manifold and a pipe exiting the ground point b from a specific second manifold whereby the pipelines of the manifolds marked at point a and point b connect to the inlet and outlet respectively of the specific portion of underground pipe where the leak is located"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_66", "text": "in the method described herein in order to detect accurately the underground leaking pipe 126 as well as to identify point a 170 and point b 172 the user 102 has to perform a series of tests involving time based temperature testing of the pipelines 114 of the manifolds 112 and a narrowing down process to pinpoint point a 170 and point b 172"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_116", "text": "generally speaking a process of elimination test may include 1 determining whether the leak is on the a cold side or a hot side 2 identifying a first test manifold 112 and identifying a first fixture 122 associated with that first test manifold 112 3 changing the temperature of the pipelines 114 of the first test manifold 112 i.e"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_116", "text": "using the time based temperature test or checking for larger sized singular pipelines 114 6 identifying a second fixture 122 7 performing another time temperature test on the pipelines of the first test manifold 112 to determine if the distribution lines 118 of the first test manifold cools down or heats up at a slower rate than the other as the distribution line 118 from the first test manifold 112 whereby the one that cools down or heats up quickly i.e"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_138", "text": "6 and the manifold 112 is before the leak 154 then both pipelines 114 would cool down after the heating element 132 is utilized to heat the pipelines 114a 114b and the fixture 122 is off because cool water is flowing through the pipelines 114a 114b to get to the leak 154 and the user 102 can detect that change in temperature the cooling of heated pipelines 114a 114b using touch 142 or temperature testing equipment 144"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_140", "text": "so after determining that the manifold 112 is before or after the leak 154 to determine which pipelines 114 are feedlines 116 or distribution lines 118 the user 102 has to perform another time temperature test with the fixture 122 turned on"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_145", "text": "by performing time based temperature checks the user 102 may determine that pipeline 114c has water flowing down towards the leak 154 at the main water entrance 108 to the property 104 and through pipeline 114c"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_155", "text": "in order for the user to determine where a distribution line 118 that has water flowing through the distribution line 118 to get to the leak 154 is going the user 102 has to temperature check each pipeline 114a 114c of the manifold 112a and compare the results one by one with each fixture 122 present on the property 104"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_165", "text": "because of this process of elimination and testing of each fixture 122 s distribution lines 118 pipelines 114a 114c from cold water manifold 112a and where the water goes the user knows that the leak 154 is past pipeline 114c from the initial temperature check and that pipeline 114c brings water to the half bath vanity sink 842 the half bath toilet 840 and hose bib 1 810 and hose bib 2 812"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_166", "text": "as noted above the process of elimination test requires the user to locate a pipeline 114 that leads to a leak 154 by performing an initial temperature test and then identify all the distribution lines 118 for any manifolds 112 of connected fixtures 112 that have water going to and from via the distribution lines 118 of the manifolds 112 and through the connected underground portions of the pipeline 114"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_169", "text": "the user 102 may perform a temperature test on pipelines 114d 114e to see if the leak 154 is past this manifold 112b or before this manifold 112b"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_170", "text": "accordingly the user may perform a temperature test on pipelines 114f 114g 114h to see if the leak 154 is past this manifold 112c as shown in fig"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_173", "text": "after opening up at these areas and performing temperature checks the user 102 tries to determine 1 if the leak 154 is past or before said manifold 112 2 which one pipeline 114 is the feedline 116 and which pipeline s 114 are the distribution lines 118 and 3 which pipelines 114 in each manifold 112 take water to other fixtures 122 on the property 104"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_175", "text": "after performing this initial temperature test the user 102 knows that pipeline 114c is a distribution line 118 and is most likely the pipeline 114 leading to the leak 154 because of the temperature change"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_209", "text": "11 18 because the hot water will be leaking out through the underground leaking cold water pipeline 126 containing an underground leak 154 which will make the surrounding area around the underground leaking cold water pipeline 126 and floors above the underground leaking cold water pipeline 126 noticeably warmer via touch 142 or temperature testing equipment 144"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_210", "text": "accordingly as the hot water returns through the underground cold water pipeline 114 from the crossover valve 184 and or supply lines 120 the user 102 should be able to detect and sense either using touch 142 or temperature testing equipment 144 that the floor of a particular location on the property 104 is noticeably warmer because the hot water will leak out through the underground cold water pipeline 114 making the floors and surrounding area located above the location of the underground leaking cold water pipeline 126 noticeably hotter as the pump 180 pumps the hot water in the loop created with the underground hot water pipelines 114 and the underground cold water pipelines 114"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_212", "text": "it may be that the user 102 finds that the floor to be marked corresponding to the underground leaking cold water pipeline 114 is in the same room areas as the installed crossover valve and test fixture 182 however it may also be true that the floor that will have a noticeable increase in temperature is located elsewhere on the property 104 and not proximate to the crossover valve 184 and installed test fixture 182"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_213", "text": "at step 1612 if the noticeable increase in the temperature of the particular floor anywhere on the property is detected the user 102 may safely assume and determine that the detectable underground cold water pipeline 114 containing the leak 154 is located beneath or near the particular floor"}, {"pat_id": "US-12442540-B1", "para_id": "para_218", "text": "at step 1626 if the noticeable increase in the temperature is detected the method may include visibly marking the particular floor associated with the noticeable increase in the temperature on the property 104 as the area of the property to be used to access the detectable 