tioning", "tube", "circuit", "shielding", "air", "value", "formula", "inside", "tm", "material", "heat", "friction", "heating", "ti", "young", "correction", "control", "connection", "operator", "case", "mass", "rti", "parallel", "facility", "unit", "shape", "energy", "hinge", "input", "damage", "inspection", "guidance", "body", "intrusion", "pressure"]</data>
  <data key="d6">["expose", "happen", "combine", "treat", "solve", "position", "inflate", "weld", "take", "assess", "connect", "look", "introduce", "base", "accord", "substitute", "indicate", "embed", "cause", "secure", "have", "ignore", "complete", "fix", "flow", "can", "dot", "restore", "pump", "output", "provide", "mention", "prevent", "give", "attach", "arrange", "correspond", "form", "measure", "harden", "eliminate", "calculate", "represent", "produce", "switch", "limit", "pass", "occur", "depend", "follow", "repair", "correct", "determine", "alternate", "cure", "bury", "step", "join", "pipeline", "discover", "convey", "lay", "stabilize", "insert", "remove", "show", "reduce", "find", "may", "discharge", "store", "detect", "design", "obtain", "separate", "dispose", "include", "perform", "operate", "open", "supply", "use", "transfer", "estimate", "know", "close", "maintain", "improve", "characterize", "shape", "hinge", "damage", "exist", "be", "utilize", "adjust", "collect", "instal", "comprise"]</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"pat_id": "JP-S5924319-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "in view of the above the present inventor has based on the assumption that it is effective to use a correlation method in order to eliminate the influence of irregular fluctuations in pressure gradient when estimating the location of a leak in a pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "JP-S5924319-B2", "para_id": "para_16", "text": "the detector for detecting the change in the pressure gradient may be a detector that measures the difference in the output of pressure gauges installed at small intervals in the pipeline 10 or a detector that detects the difference in the output of pressure gauges installed at small intervals in the pipeline 10 or a detector that measures the differential pressure at positions separated by small intervals in the pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "KR-102240942-B1", "para_id": "claim_1", "text": "a pipeline survey step in which the operator determines whether or not and its location and in the case of a pipeline with a diameter larger than that of a small diameter pipeline the operator determines whether there is damage intrusion water the gradient of the pipeline the presence of obstacles and the corresponding location in the pipeline survey step an obstacle removal operation is included depending on the presence or absence of an obstacle and an obstacle removal dredging and washing step of dredging and washing in a small diameter pipe turning water to prevent the inflow of water into the small diameter pipe dry tube arranging step of arranging the dry tube in the small diameter pipe to be repaired a dry tube curing step of inflating the dry tube to the dry tube disposed in the pipeline to supply compressed air for maintaining the shape and steam for curing the dry tube to harden it and a construction completion step of completing the construction by arranging the dry tube at the location of the connection pipe so that the connection pipe connected to the small diameter pipe and the small diameter pipe can be connected after the dry tube hardening step"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101008852-B1", "para_id": "claim_1", "text": "in the sewage pipe installed with the switchgear to discharge the clear rainwater to the pipeline and collect only the initial rainwater and the sewage into the rainwater discharge chamber a pair of fixing brackets fixed to both sides of an exposed cross section of the sewage pipe a first tube welded to the front lower surface of each fixing bracket a shielding plate shaped to correspond to the shape of the exposed cross sectional size of the sewage pipe a second pipe body welded to both sides of the upper surface of the shielding plate and and a hinge shaft inserted between the first pipe and the second pipe so that the screen plate is hinged to an upper portion of the exposed end of the sewage pipe so as to open and close the open exposed cross section of the sewage pipe"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101946554-B1", "para_id": "claim_8", "text": "the sewage treatment plant 130 processes the sewage to remove the treated water producing stage d the treated water produced by the sewage treatment plant 130 is stored in the treated water storage tank 120b of the second storage tank 120 and the treated water is supplied to the first storage tank 110 to a supply water storage tank 110b of the storage tank 110 e supplying the supply water stored in the supply water storage tank 110b of the first storage vessel 110 to the city water or the river maintenance water through the water supply pump 190 f if the water level is greater than the reference value the supply water storage tank 110b of the first storage tank 110 is switched to the inflow sewage storage tank 110a and the process water reuse pipe 150 is switched to the sewage transfer pipe 140 g the wastewater flowing through the combined sewage line 200 is stored in the inflow sewage storage tank 110a and the supply water storage tank 110b as stormwater and wastewater and the sewage transfer pipeline 140 and the treatment water pipeline 150 transferring the storm water and the sewage water respectively h transferring the treated water storage tank 120b of the second storage tank 120 to the transfer sewage storage tank 120a and storing storm water and sewage water in the transfer sewage storage tank 120a and the treated water storage tank 120b and i discharging stormwater and sewage stored in the transfer sewage storage tank 120a and the treated water storage tank 120b to the sewage treatment plant and treating the sewage treatment plant 130 with stormwater and sewage lt rti the treated water recycling duct 150 may be embedded in parallel on the side of the sewage conveying pipe 140 so as to be utilized in a region having a small geographical radial gradient in order to secure the water supply end face of the combined sewage pipe 200 at the time of rainfall characterized in that the water supply and the flow control are adjustable"}, {"pat_id": "KR-100338306-B1", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "conventionally in order to reduce the flow velocity of the pipeline the pipeline is laid with a gentle gradient and a drop hole or manhole is installed in the middle to adjust the step by step and in case of a cast in place concrete facility such as a box type culvert it is designed to have a small step inside the culvert to prevent crushing of the facility"}, {"pat_id": "KR-200338809-Y1", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "the existing sewage pipe is installed by giving a gradient between the manhole and the manhole not a pressure pipe such as a water supply pipe or a gas pipeline and since sewage flows due to its natural gradient the sewage pipe is damaged in the underground state or the excellent pipe is joined to the sewage pipe"}, {"pat_id": "US-11313755-B2", "para_id": "para_53", "text": "in some examples pipeline conditions determined and stored in block 802 above include but are not limited to pipeline internal diameter pipeline wall thickness pipeline material young s modulus pipeline length friction factor pipeline material type topography temperature gradient pressure gradient and unusual features if any"}, {"pat_id": "US-11263374-B2", "para_id": "para_84", "text": "in some implementations one or both of the total mass rate and the pressure gradient for example from table 1 can be used to determine the calculated leak size"}, {"pat_id": "JP-S5924320-B2", "para_id": "claim_1", "text": "a pipeline in which pressure gradient detectors are attached to the upstream and downstream sides and a device for detecting the occurrence of leakage in the pipeline is connected to these detectors the device comprising based on the output difference of the above detector the leakage detection index leak is calculated as leak nln 2_e 2m however n number of data 2_e error variance m leakage detection index calculation circuit calculated based on the order of the filter and the leakage detection index leak obtained as the output of this calculation circuit is used to determine whether or not a leak has occurred"}, {"pat_id": "JP-S5924320-B2", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "in this case as a first step it is necessary to promptly detect the occurrence of a leak and this is a prerequisite for discovering the leak location"}, {"pat_id": "JP-S5924320-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "the above mentioned pressure gradient detector may be a detector that detects the difference in the output of pressure gauges installed at small intervals in the pipeline 10 or a differential pressure detector that detects the differential pressure at positions separated by a small interval in the pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "US-12235188-B2", "para_id": "claim_1", "text": "tm ti h ti to cp \u03c1 \u03b4 wherein ti is a temperature of fluid in the pipeline and the unit is k h is a convection heat transfer coefficient of steam and a thermal insulation layer 6 in the pipeline and the unit is w m2 k cp is a specific heat capacity of steam and the unit is j kg k \u03c1 is a steam density and the unit is kg m3 \u03b4 is a wall thickness of the pipeline namely a distance by which steam flows out from the inside of the pipeline via a leakage hole to the outer wall of the pipeline in unit time and the unit is m s \u03b4 mt\u03c1 a wherein m is a mass flow of steam namely a fluid mass of the fluid that passes through an effective cross section of a closed pipeline in unit time and the unit is kg s t is a unit time namely 1 s and a is the area of the leakage hole and the unit is m2 a corresponding relationship between a steam pressure and a temperature in the heating pipeline is obtained by an antoine empirical formula or by looking up a table of steam pressure and temperature enthalpy values as shown in a formula 7 below ln p 9.3876 3826.36t 45.47 7 wherein p is the steam pressure and the unit is mpa t is the steam temperature and the unit is k a value p calculated by substituting tm as the steam temperature into the formula 7 is the pipeline leakage transient pressure pm corresponding to the pipeline leakage transient steam temperature tm in step 3 the process of positioning a leakage location by the plc according to a pressure gradient method is specifically as follows firstly a pressure gradient p x leftbracketingbar u upstream of a leakage point and a pressure gradient p x leftbracketingbar d downstream of the leakage point are calculated p x leftbracketingbar u p 1 pmx 8 p x leftbracketingbar d pm p 2l s x 9 wherein l is a distance from a pressure sensor within a region of the leakage point to a temperature sensor downstream thereof and the unit is m s is a distance from a temperature sensor within the region of the leakage point to a pressure sensor downstream thereof and the unit is m x is a distance from the location of the leakage point to a pressure sensor upstream within the region of the leakage point and the unit is m x represents a gradient direction dot over p 1 is a pressure value detected by the pressure sensor upstream of the leakage point and the unit is mpa dot over p 2 is a pressure value detected by a pressure sensor downstream of the leakage point and the unit is mpa and pm is the pipeline leakage transient pressure and the unit is mpa it is known from a formula for conservation of momentum within a steam pipeline p x leftbracketingbar u f \u03c1 u22 d 10 p x leftbracketingbar d f \u03c1 u22 d wherein f is a friction coefficient u is a flow velocity of steam and the unit is m s \u03c1 is a steam density and the unit is kg m3 d is a pipeline diameter and the unit is m the aperture of a leakage hole is less than the pipeline diameter and a pipeline length and the variation of friction coefficients of two adjacent sides of the leakage hole within a short distance are ignored in the case of pipeline leakage no phenomena of obvious free outflow and flow reduction of steam occur because of the presence of the thermal insulation layer 6 and therefore pressure gradients upstream and downstream of the leakage point are approximately the same and after being stabilized the pressures restore an original pressure distribution along the pipeline it can be obtained from simultaneous equations 8 10 that x l s p 1 pm p 1 p 2 since the arrangement locations of the sensors are known the location of the leakage point can be positioned accurately according to a calculated value x and in step 3 in the process of positioning the location of the leakage point a correction coefficient \u03be is introduced and the value x is corrected by a method of using temperatures to correct pressures wherein the correction coefficient is represented by a formula below \u03be 2 tmt 1 t 2 a corrected new value x is x l s p 1 pm p 1 p 2 \u03be wherein tm is the pipeline leakage transient steam temperature and the unit is k dot over t 1 is a temperature value detected by the temperature sensor upstream of the leakage point and the unit is k and dot over t 2 is a temperature value detected by the temperature sensor downstream of the leakage point and the unit is k"}, {"pat_id": "US-12235188-B2", "para_id": "para_55", "text": "2 performing energy analysis on fluid at the leakage location of the pipeline to obtain the leakage transient temperature taking a temperature variation as guidance for solving the pressure gradient positioning the leakage point of the heating pipeline improving positioning accuracy performing logical operation via the plc by the algorithm finding the leakage in time and outputting early warning signals"}, {"pat_id": "US-12235188-B2", "para_id": "para_132", "text": "when an on off event happens to the heating supply pump unit at the starting end of the pipeline a great gradient variation of the pressure in the pipeline may be caused which can cause a false leakage early warning easily therefore the plc collects data from the pressure sensors and the temperature sensors to form a pressure dataset p1 p2 p3 p4"}, {"pat_id": "EP-4204662-B1", "para_id": "para_13", "text": "each of the one or more inspection tools may provide respective quantitative measurements indicating a severity level of a degradation of the external coating on the exterior surface of the buried pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "EP-4204662-B1", "para_id": "para_29", "text": "while utilizing cathodic protection current and above ground inspection tool to assess the degradation of the protective coating some implementations operate on for example six data inputs namely coating type soil resistivity cathodic protection current metal loss corrosion close interval survey and alternating current voltage gradient or direct current voltage gradient to perform a comprehensive analysis of the external protective coating of a buried pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "EP-4204662-B1", "para_id": "para_40", "text": "hence the output result has no indication of metal loss"}]</data>
</edge>
